#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148335} :activity/id #uuid "982ccfc9-c613-5d9e-8291-be7273b41b36" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049083 :topic/id #uuid "d9335c65-9755-52f0-8ecc-68ac966800f0" :topic/math-academy-id 2534} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148692 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a *positive* geometric sequence where the second term is $4$ and the fourth term is $64.$  How do we find the common ratio $r?$\n\nThis is a little trickier than before because we're not given two consecutive terms, but let's try to figure it out. First, let's denote\n\n$$\na_2 = 4, \\qquad a_4 = 64.\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by:\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=2$ and $n=4$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{2-1}={a}_{1}r \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing that $a_2 = 4$ and $a_4 = 64$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}r & =4 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{3} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have a system of equations to solve. To solve this system, we *divide* the *second* equation above by the *first* equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}}{{a}_{1}r} & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{{a}_{1}}{r}^{3}}{\\cancel{{a}_{1}}r} & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{3}}{r} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r.$ Note that $16 = 4^2,$ and so we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since our sequence must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio $r=4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "37612557-7324-573c-b33f-5503374cbd61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2013 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c5a853ac-bca8-588c-9552-d62691b2b1cb" :step/math-academy-id 456842 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147230 :question/id #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554621}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"} :step/id #uuid "a9dbb1d0-1aa6-5b1b-8d37-cd6baf4d3f42" :step/math-academy-id 456843 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148693 :tutorial/content "Finding the common ratio by solving a system of equations is quite a lot of work. Is there an easier way?\n\nIndeed there is. Given two terms of a geometric sequence $a_n$ and $a_m,$ the formula for $r$ is as follows:\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works. Suppose that we have a geometric sequence whose second term is $3$ and whose fifth term is $192.$ What is the common ratio?\n\nHere, we're given $a_5 = 192$ and $a_2 = 3.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{192}{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r$ by noting that $64 = 4^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={4}^{3} \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the common ratio is $r=4.$ \n\nWhen dealing with an equation that is **cubic** in $r$ (i.e., contains an $r^3$), then there is only one possible answer, unlike the squared case ($r^2$) where we have to pick from either positive or negative." :tutorial/id #uuid "e368a435-c6f1-5ff3-b7dc-76122c25a574" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2015 :tutorial/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula"} :step/id #uuid "8d4fd6a5-b7a1-5431-ac86-c98ca494cd68" :step/math-academy-id 456846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147226 :question/id #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2175"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050828}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554612}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "376f6145-6352-593b-bfb2-743f3f279fe9" :step/math-academy-id 456847 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147229 :question/id #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554633}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "29dd91b7-618d-590b-82d4-ba8d0996e7fe" :step/math-academy-id 456850}] :activity/title "Finding the Common Ratio of a Geometric Sequence Given Two Terms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147713}]}
